# What is there to learn about thinking with agents?

**The question.** Learn as much as possible in the process, next to the newly
developed dynamic formalism for interacting with AI agents. This is the fifth
of the five questions in the project README.

## Where the answer stands

The part of the discussion that left strange loops behind is the part that
answers this. It began from the remark that mathematics is full of
"connections" that come before recursion, and ended as a map of the
operations a mind or a model performs.

**Three layers.** The map sorts everything discussed into three levels.

1. *Operations*: iteration, inversion, induction, abstraction, generation.
2. *Structural relationships*: duality, invariance, equivalence, fixed
   points, reconstruction.
3. *Reflective phenomena*: self-description, diagonalization, semantic
   limits, reflective revision.

Strange loops belong to the third layer. Most of what an agent does belongs to
the first.

**Seventeen operations.** For work mediated by a language model the first
layer is spelled out as seventeen operations, each with an input and an
output. The list was corrected once in the discussion: induction had been left
out and was restored beside deduction and abduction.

**Distinctions that were insisted on.** The lessons are mostly of the form
"these two are not the same".

- Abstraction is not summarization. Thinking abstractly finds structure
  independent of detail. Writing an abstract condenses a text.
- Induction is not generalization. One infers from evidence, the other widens
  a claim.
- Induction as inference is not mathematical induction, which is deductive.
- Generation is the little brother of generalization and not a form of it.
- Duality is not self-reference. Two descriptions can reconstruct each other
  with no loop.
- An analogy is not an isomorphism. It preserves selected relations only, and
  has to be tested for where it breaks.

**Operations come in cycles.** No operation is useful alone. Induction and
abduction give hypotheses, deduction gives predictions, testing gives
observations. Compressing observations gives a model, the model predicts, and
a second loop builds a model of the modelling itself.

**What stays conjecture.** That compression is intelligence. That every
sufficiently reflective mind meets its own incompleteness. That a description
of the whole can never be the whole, as the opening of the *Tao Te Ching* has
it. The discussion keeps all three as analogies and proves none.

